## 1 · Aggregate and value objects

**Aggregate root:** `User`. Boundary: the user and their email. `Department` enters by
**reference (id)**, not composition — it's another aggregate, and a user doesn't control
it.

| Field | Type | Why this type |
|---|---|---|
| `id` | `UserId` | Value object over `UUID`. Prevents swapping a user id for an order id in a signature |
| `email` | `Email` | Concentrates format validation in one place. `String` spread it across controller, service, and test |
| `name` | `String` | No rule of its own beyond length, which `User`'s constructor checks |
| `departmentId` | `Optional<DepartmentId>` | Optional in the payload. `Optional` on a query return is allowed; here it's a field — **see note** |
| `status` | `UserStatus` | Closed set — an `enum`, not a value object (`@.claude/rules/value-objects.md`). Its values and what reaches each are in **State reachability** below |

> **Compliance note.** `@.claude/rules/code-quality.md` forbids `Optional` on a field.
> Resolution: the field is `DepartmentId departmentId`, possibly absent, modeled as a
> sealed `UserDepartment` (`Assigned` \| `Unassigned`), or the field is mandatory and the
> default is made explicit. Decided: **mandatory field with `DepartmentId.unassigned()`**
> — no `null`, no `Optional` on a field.

**Value objects**

| VO | Wraps | Own invariant | Factory |
|---|---|---|---|
| `UserId` | `UUID` | not null | `UserId.of(UUID)` · `UserId.newId()` |
| `Email` | `String` | valid format, ≤ 254 characters, normalized to lowercase | `Email.of(String)` |
| `DepartmentId` | `UUID` | not null | `DepartmentId.of(UUID)` · `DepartmentId.unassigned()` |

All `record`, immutable, with validation in the compact constructor and a non-public
constructor. Shape: `templates/ValueObject.java.example`.

```mermaid
classDiagram
    direction LR
    class User {
        UserId id
        Email email
        String name
        DepartmentId departmentId
        UserStatus status
    }
    class UserStatus {
        <<enumeration>>
        PENDING_EMAIL_CONFIRMATION
        ACTIVE
        DEACTIVATED
    }
    User *-- UserId
    User *-- Email
    User *-- DepartmentId : Department by reference
    User *-- UserStatus
```

The root with the fields of the table above, its value objects, and `Department` by its id
only — `@.claude/rules/spec-diagrams.md`.

**State reachability** — written whenever a field's type admits more than one value; `none`
when the aggregate has no state field. Every value some use case will need is reachable
through the aggregate's own API, or a named case is on the hook for it.

| State field | Value | Reached by | Status |
|---|---|---|---|
| `status` | `PENDING_EMAIL_CONFIRMATION` | `User.register(...)` — the creation factory | this case |
| `status` | `ACTIVE` | `User.confirmEmail(token, clock)` | this case |
| `status` | `DEACTIVATED` | nothing yet — `UC-007-deactivate-user` (backlog) | named future case |

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> PENDING_EMAIL_CONFIRMATION : register
    PENDING_EMAIL_CONFIRMATION --> ACTIVE : confirmEmail
    DEACTIVATED
    note right of DEACTIVATED : UC-007-deactivate-user (backlog)
```

The state diagram is drawn from this table, and only when the table is not `none`: one state
per value, one transition per method in *Reached by*, a note on a value nothing reaches yet.

The reidratation path is **not** a row here. A factory that rebuilds persisted state
(`rehydrate`, the constructor the mapper calls) is not a transition, and a value reachable only
through it is reachable in tests and unreachable in production — which is how a later case's
precondition on `ACTIVE` turned every real request into a 422 while the build stayed green. A
value with no method and no named case is the gap this block exists to prevent.

## 2 · Invariants

| # | Invariant | Where it's enforced | Exception | `errorCode` |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `email` has a valid format | `Email` compact constructor | `ValidationException` | `EMAIL_INVALID` |
| 2 | `email` has ≤ 254 characters | `Email` compact constructor | `ValidationException` | `EMAIL_TOO_LONG` |
| 3 | `name` has 2 to 120 characters | `User` compact constructor | `ValidationException` | `USER_NAME_LENGTH` |
| 4 | no required field is null | each record's compact constructor | `ValidationException` | `<FIELD>_REQUIRED` |
| 5 | `email` is unique in the system | **outside the aggregate** — the output port checks, and the constraint arbitrates | `ConflictException` | `EMAIL_ALREADY_USED` |

Invariant 5 doesn't fit in the aggregate: it requires knowing the other users, and an
aggregate doesn't query the repository. It lives in the input port, before building the
`User`, and the `UNIQUE` constraint closes the race — see `20-persistencia.md`.

No generic JDK exception. `@.claude/rules/error-handling.md`.

## 3 · Ports

**Input** — `application/port/in/user/CreateUserUseCase.java`

```
interface CreateUserUseCase
    UserId create(CreateUserCommand command)
```

One method, one use case. Throws `ValidationException` (invariants 1-4, propagated from
the constructors) and `ConflictException` (invariant 5).

**Command** — `application/port/in/user/CreateUserCommand.java`

```
record CreateUserCommand(String email, String name, UUID departmentId)
```

Primitive and JDK types at the input: the command is the boundary where the outside
world hasn't been validated yet. It's the implementation that builds the value objects
and lets the invariants fire. Zero framework types — no `jakarta.validation`, which
lives in the adapter's DTO.

**Output** — `application/port/out/UserRepository.java` · kind `persistence`

```
interface UserRepository
    boolean existsByEmail(Email email)
    User save(User user)
```

`existsByEmail` serves invariant 5. `save` returns the persisted aggregate — the id may
be generated by the database. No JPA, Spring, or SQL types in the signatures.

Every output port names its kind — `persistence` · `messaging` · `external HTTP` · `LLM` ·
`vector store` · `search index` — next to its path. The kind is what decides which layer partial
implements it: a port of kind `external HTTP` is what makes `/new-feature` run
`http-client-architect`, one of kind `LLM` or `vector store` runs `ai-architect`, one of kind
`search index` is implemented from `23-busca.md` (`search-architect`, run because
`00-caso-de-uso.md` names a search index), and a port with no kind is a question the orchestrator
has to come back with.

**Implementation** — `application/service/user/CreateUserService.java`. Implements
`CreateUserUseCase`. Constructor injection, `private final` fields. This is where the
transaction opens and closes (`@.claude/rules/architecture-ddd.md`, Application section);
the annotation lives on this class, which is application layer, not domain.
Names per the active blueprint's vocabulary (`naming.md` § Architecture vocabulary): this
exemplar is hexagonal. In clean architecture there's no port and no `Service` — the
concrete `CreateUserUseCase` is the implementation.

## 4 · Events

| Event | Payload | Emitted when | Consumed by | Durability |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `UserCreated` | `UserId`, `Email`, `Instant occurredAt` | `User` built and persisted successfully | **UC-002** — welcome email | loss tolerable — a missing welcome email is visible to the user and re-sendable by hand |

Past-tense name. Immutable `record`, no framework types. `occurredAt` comes from an
injected `Clock`, never `Instant.now()` — `@.claude/rules/architecture-ddd.md`.

The event is what makes splitting UC-001/UC-002 possible: without it, sending the email
would have to live inside this use case, which is exactly what the mother spec rejected.

Publishing and transport aren't part of this file. Who publishes and with what guarantee
is a `messaging-architect` decision.

`Durability` is the exception, and it is a domain property, not a transport one: whether the
absence of this event is later detectable and recoverable (**loss tolerable**), or whether
losing it corrupts state a human then has to reconcile (**must not be lost** — money moved,
an external ledger, a regulatory record, or an effect nothing else would ever notice missing).
Two words and the reason; `messaging-architect` reads this column and picks the publication
form from it, and writing the form here instead is deciding outside this file's territory.

